  {
   "id": "c2",
   "kind": "concept",
   "body": "The coefficients add or subtract; the radical is a label that must match. 4√2 + 3√2 = 7√2, but 4√2 + 3√5 stays as it is — different radicals can't be merged, just like 4x + 3y can't.",
   "figure": "rad-like-radicals"
  },
  {
   "id": "i2",
   "kind": "interactive",
   "body": "Which pair combines?",
   "widget": {
    "type": "mcq",
    "prompt": "Which expression can be combined into a single term?",
    "options": [
     {
      "id": "a",
      "label": "√7 + 3√7",
      "correct": true,
      "feedback": "Yes — same radicand √7, so 1 + 3 = 4√7."
     },
     {
      "id": "b",
      "label": "√2 + √3",
      "feedback": "Different radicands (2 and 3), so these can't be combined."
     },
     {
      "id": "c",
      "label": "2√5 + 2√6",
      "feedback": "Different radicands (5 and 6), so these can't be combined."
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "kind": "concept",
   "body": "Sometimes you must **simplify first** to reveal like radicals. √8 + √2 looks unlike, but √8 = 2√2, so it becomes 2√2 + √2 = 3√2. Simplify each radical, then combine the ones that match.",
   "figure": "rad-simplify-first"
  },
